-- Uzbekistan deep-dive content (English). A mature, universal B2B
-- e-invoicing regime (ESF / электрон счёт-фактура): voluntary from
-- 1 Jul 2019, mandatory for all business entities since 1 Jan 2020,
-- and since 1 Jan 2026 layered with real-time automated risk scoring
-- that ties the buyer's input VAT credit to the invoice's risk status.
--
-- Deliberate editorial position on the compliance model: this page
-- does NOT call Uzbekistan a clearance regime, despite one circulated
-- industry booklet doing so. The evidence points the other way and is
-- set out in its own card -- the buyer, not the tax authority, is the
-- validation gate, and the 2026 risk system colours invoices rather
-- than blocking them. Where sources genuinely disagree the page says
-- so rather than picking a tidy label.
--
-- Sourcing: lex.uz (Resolutions 522, 489, 168; Decree UP-153; Law
-- ZRU-1108; Decree UP-100) and gov.uz (State Tax Committee) fetched
-- directly; Uzbek specialist legal press (Norma.uz, Buxgalter.uz,
-- Gazeta.uz, Spot.uz, UzDaily) for operational detail and statistics;
-- EY and PwC as labelled secondary. Flagged gaps are stated on the
-- page itself rather than hidden: Appendix 2 to Resolution 489 could
-- not be retrieved in full, Tax Code Article 223's verbatim text was
-- not read directly, the JSON format rests on secondary sources only,
-- and B2C scope is genuinely contested.

  'Centralised continuous transaction control (CTC): universal B2B e-invoicing since 2020, exchanged through the state platform or a registered operator, with the buyer as the validation gate — not a pre-clearance regime',
  'This deep-dive page reflects Uzbek legislation as published on lex.uz and the State Tax Committee''s own portal (gov.uz), supplemented by Uzbek specialist legal and tax press where primary appendices could not be retrieved in full, as of 10 August 2026. It is provided for general awareness, not legal or tax advice. Several items are explicitly flagged on this page as unconfirmed rather than presented as settled — please read those notes. Confirm current requirements directly with Uzbekistan''s Tax Committee, your ESF operator, or an Uzbek tax adviser before relying on this page for compliance decisions.',
  'Uzbekistan''s ESF electronic invoice regime was created by Cabinet of Ministers Resolution No. 522 of 25 June 2019: voluntary from 1 July 2019, mandatory for all business entities from 1 January 2020. Resolution No. 489 of August 2020 set the operating rules still in force. Two changes landed on 1 January 2026 — real-time invoice risk scoring, and the extension of the obligation to the self-employed.',
  'A compliant Uzbek e-invoice is a structured electronic document signed with an ETsP digital signature by the seller and countersigned by the buyer on acceptance. It is exchanged either through the state platform or through any of the registered private EDO operators, with interoperability between operators handled by the state-designated roaming operator, the state enterprise "Yangi Texnologiyalar". Product and service lines carry ITSPU classification codes drawn from the national e-catalogue.',
  'The obligation is universal for business entities and is not tied to the VAT threshold: every economic entity must issue electronic invoices, whether or not it is a VAT payer. Self-employed persons were brought in from 1 January 2026. Paper invoices survive only where a transaction involves state secrets. Sales to individual consumers sit outside the electronic exchange loop in practice and are covered by online cash registers instead.',
  'If you trade B2B in Uzbekistan you are already in scope and have been since 2020 — there is no phase-in left to prepare for. The live work is adapting to the two January 2026 changes: the risk-scoring system, which affects when your buyers can recover input VAT on your invoices, and the pre-filled returns generated from your own invoice feed.',
  'Uzbekistan backs the mandate with a single quantified sanction — 20% of the concealed tax base under Tax Code Article 223 — rather than a published schedule of fixed fines. The 2026 risk-scoring system carries no penalty at all; its consequence is deferral of the buyer''s input VAT credit.',
  'Uzbekistan has required electronic invoices from all business entities since 1 January 2020, under Cabinet of Ministers Resolution No. 522. Invoices are exchanged through the state platform or a registered private operator, digitally signed by the seller and confirmed by the buyer within ten days. From 1 January 2026 every invoice is scored for risk in real time, with input VAT on a flagged invoice creditable only once the tax reaches the budget, and the tax authority now pre-fills VAT returns directly from the invoice feed.',
  '🇺🇿'
FROM countries WHERE code = 'UZ';








